  "prompt_text": "The figure is a cross-section of the ocean that links marine habitats with depth and available light. It starts at the shore with the intertidal zone and continental shelf, showing estuarine and coastal environments near land, then extends far offshore into the pelagic zone and down to the abyssal seafloor. A depth scale runs from the surface to about 10,000 metres, while a color gradient marks the transition from light near the surface to twilight and then complete darkness.\n\nThe main message is that different kinds of marine life occupy distinct parts of this vertical and horizontal gradient. Near the coast and surface, organisms such as seaweed, a turtle, coral-like benthic life, large whales, jellyfish, and shallow fish are shown in well-lit water. Around a few hundred metres, larger predators and schooling fish appear in the dimmer twilight zone. Deeper still, the seafloor slope and open water host squid, shrimp-like animals, and deeper-bodied fishes. In the abyssal darkness, unusual deep-sea species such as an anglerfish and an eel-like fish are illustrated near the bottom. Overall, the image emphasizes how ocean biodiversity is structured by depth, distance from shore, and diminishing light.",
